WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:01.201
Now turn to Congresswoman

00:00:01.201 --> 00:00:02.502
and Progressive Caucus Chair

00:00:02.502 --> 00:00:03.403
Pramila Jayapal,

00:00:03.403 --> 00:00:04.504
who has had concerns

00:00:04.504 --> 00:00:05.572
about some of the measures

00:00:05.572 --> 00:00:07.140
that end up in this bill.

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:07.974
Congressman, thanks so much.

00:00:07.974 --> 00:00:08.942
Appreciate it.

00:00:08.942 --> 00:00:10.710
Congresswoman, so the specific

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:11.611
legislative language

00:00:11.611 --> 00:00:13.213
still being worked on.

00:00:13.213 --> 00:00:13.613
President

00:00:13.613 --> 00:00:14.514
Biden says this agreement

00:00:14.514 --> 00:00:15.315
is a compromise,

00:00:15.315 --> 00:00:16.316
which means that everyone gets it.

00:00:16.316 --> 00:00:16.583
They want

00:00:16.583 --> 00:00:17.050
you just heard

00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:18.451
Congressman Johnson say

00:00:18.451 --> 00:00:19.085
Democrats didn't

00:00:19.085 --> 00:00:21.154
get anything in the deal.

00:00:21.154 --> 00:00:21.988
Are you going to support that?

00:00:21.988 --> 00:00:22.789
He also said that

00:00:22.789 --> 00:00:23.089
he thinks

00:00:23.089 --> 00:00:23.990
you're going to vote against it.

00:00:23.990 --> 00:00:25.558
How are you going to vote

00:00:26.993 --> 00:00:28.628
Well, I don't know yet, Jake,

00:00:28.628 --> 00:00:30.296
because I haven't seen the text.

00:00:30.296 --> 00:00:30.597
You know,

00:00:30.597 --> 00:00:33.433
I'm not a big fan of in principle

00:00:33.433 --> 00:00:34.334
or frameworks.

00:00:34.334 --> 00:00:36.202
That's always

00:00:36.202 --> 00:00:37.170
you know, a problem

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:38.605
if you can't see the exact

00:00:38.605 --> 00:00:39.672
legislative text.

00:00:39.672 --> 00:00:40.440
And we're all trying

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:41.708
to wade through spend right now.

00:00:41.708 --> 00:00:43.610
I think that's certainly what you heard

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:44.978
from my good colleague

00:00:44.978 --> 00:00:45.545
on the other side of

00:00:45.545 --> 00:00:47.747
the aisle is a lot of spin.

00:00:47.747 --> 00:00:49.215
But I think it's going to come down

00:00:49.215 --> 00:00:50.750
to what the legislative text is.

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:52.352
I think at the end of the day, though,

00:00:52.352 --> 00:00:54.387
the American people have to understand

00:00:54.387 --> 00:00:56.523
that we are at the brink of default.

00:00:56.523 --> 00:00:58.191
We have we don't have a deal yet.

00:00:58.191 --> 00:00:58.892
We're not sure

00:00:58.892 --> 00:00:59.859
how many Republican votes

00:00:59.859 --> 00:01:00.960
can be produced.

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:03.930
And it is all because Republicans said

00:01:04.164 --> 00:01:06.032
that they wanted to cut the deficit.

00:01:06.032 --> 00:01:07.600
And let's be clear

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:10.503
that what they got from this was not that

00:01:11.071 --> 00:01:12.472
they may have gotten other things.

00:01:12.472 --> 00:01:13.740
I'm not happy

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:14.574
with some of the things

00:01:14.574 --> 00:01:15.675
I'm hearing about,

00:01:15.675 --> 00:01:18.278
but they are not cutting the deficit

00:01:18.912 --> 00:01:20.513
and they are not cutting spending.

00:01:20.513 --> 00:01:22.782
Because if you think about the fact

00:01:22.782 --> 00:01:24.551
that they've agreed to increase Pentagon

00:01:24.551 --> 00:01:26.019
spending, Number one,

00:01:26.019 --> 00:01:28.388
they've agreed to increase VA spending.

00:01:28.388 --> 00:01:29.456
Number two,

00:01:29.456 --> 00:01:32.425
and while there are some fiscal

00:01:33.159 --> 00:01:34.861
calculations that are being made around

00:01:34.861 --> 00:01:35.662
what non-defense

00:01:35.662 --> 00:01:36.996
discretionary spending is,

00:01:36.996 --> 00:01:37.897
and by the way, for people

00:01:37.897 --> 00:01:38.898
that are listening to that,

00:01:38.898 --> 00:01:40.266
that's a lot of mumbo jumbo.

00:01:40.266 --> 00:01:41.034
That's basic

00:01:41.034 --> 00:01:42.602
spending on things like health care,

00:01:42.602 --> 00:01:44.237
education, childcare,

00:01:44.237 --> 00:01:46.072
all the things you care about

00:01:46.072 --> 00:01:48.341
is what Republicans want to cut.

00:01:48.608 --> 00:01:52.412
And they even took back $10 billion from

00:01:52.645 --> 00:01:53.546
the IRS.

00:01:53.546 --> 00:01:53.813
That was

00:01:53.813 --> 00:01:56.416
supposed to go to taking on wealthy

00:01:56.416 --> 00:01:57.550
tax cheats

00:01:57.550 --> 00:02:00.253
in order to make regular Americans

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:03.490
pay for wealthy people.

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:05.492
To be able to continue to get tax breaks.

00:02:05.492 --> 00:02:08.528
So I think that you got to ask yourself,

00:02:08.761 --> 00:02:11.064
what was all the drama for?

00:02:11.064 --> 00:02:13.466
Because they didn't get what they said

00:02:13.466 --> 00:02:14.000
they wanted.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:15.535
We knew that was never

00:02:15.535 --> 00:02:16.970
actually what was on the table.

00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:18.238
So the deal, as you know,

00:02:18.238 --> 00:02:19.472
expands food stamp

00:02:19.472 --> 00:02:21.007
work requirements for able

00:02:21.007 --> 00:02:22.709
bodied adults under 55

00:02:22.709 --> 00:02:23.843
who do not have dependents.

00:02:23.843 --> 00:02:25.478
This goes throughout 2030.

00:02:25.478 --> 00:02:26.913
This excludes veterans

00:02:26.913 --> 00:02:28.882
and those experiencing homelessness.

00:02:28.882 --> 00:02:30.817
You have said that your caucus

00:02:30.817 --> 00:02:32.652
would not support a bill

00:02:32.652 --> 00:02:34.020
with work requirements

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:35.822
that, quote, hurt poor people.

00:02:35.822 --> 00:02:37.357
Does this deal do that?

00:02:37.357 --> 00:02:39.159
Is this a deal breaker for you?

00:02:41.194 --> 00:02:42.662
I don't know because I

00:02:42.662 --> 00:02:44.464
haven't seen the language.

00:02:44.464 --> 00:02:46.065
And what?

00:02:46.065 --> 00:02:47.667
First of all, let me say.

00:02:47.667 --> 00:02:49.435
Terrible policy.

00:02:49.435 --> 00:02:50.537
Absolutely terrible.

00:02:50.537 --> 00:02:53.239
Policy does not reduce spending actually,

00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:54.674
by some estimates,

00:02:54.674 --> 00:02:55.275
creates

00:02:55.275 --> 00:02:57.210
a burden on administrative

00:02:57.210 --> 00:02:59.679
spending that is actually worse for,

00:02:59.812 --> 00:03:00.180
you know,

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:01.881
for the overall cost of a program

00:03:01.881 --> 00:03:02.448
like that.

00:03:02.448 --> 00:03:03.483
Number two,

00:03:03.483 --> 00:03:06.085
it is about people who are hungry,

00:03:06.286 --> 00:03:08.087
people who just need a little bit

00:03:08.087 --> 00:03:09.189
of temporary assistance

00:03:09.189 --> 00:03:12.692
and we are one of the only countries

00:03:12.692 --> 00:03:13.126
in the world.

00:03:13.126 --> 00:03:14.794
It's not the only country in the world

00:03:14.794 --> 00:03:16.863
that is an industrialized country

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:19.065
that puts any requirements on people

00:03:19.065 --> 00:03:19.966
who just want food.

00:03:19.966 --> 00:03:22.802
So very bad policy does not save money

00:03:22.802 --> 00:03:25.271
and, by the way, does not work.

00:03:25.271 --> 00:03:27.207
We've seen reams of data

00:03:27.207 --> 00:03:27.707
that show

00:03:27.707 --> 00:03:29.175
that when you put these work requirements

00:03:29.175 --> 00:03:31.578
in, they're really just administrative

00:03:31.578 --> 00:03:32.245
red tape

00:03:32.245 --> 00:03:34.247
that prevent the people who need help

00:03:34.247 --> 00:03:35.848
from getting help.

00:03:35.848 --> 00:03:38.284
What I'm not sure on and what I'm

00:03:38.284 --> 00:03:39.419
looking at right now

00:03:39.419 --> 00:03:41.454
and I need to see the legislative text

00:03:41.454 --> 00:03:43.356
is what it means

00:03:43.356 --> 00:03:44.991
in terms of the exemptions

00:03:44.991 --> 00:03:48.828
that were put in for veterans, for folks

00:03:48.828 --> 00:03:50.163
who are experiencing homelessness,

00:03:50.163 --> 00:03:50.697
for people

00:03:50.697 --> 00:03:52.765
who are coming out of foster care.

00:03:52.765 --> 00:03:54.334
Those are three exemptions

00:03:54.334 --> 00:03:55.568
that were included.

00:03:55.568 --> 00:03:57.370
And so what are the numbers look like

00:03:57.370 --> 00:03:58.238
at the end of the day?

00:03:58.238 --> 00:03:59.305
I'm not sure.

00:03:59.305 --> 00:04:01.741
However, it is bad policy.

00:04:01.741 --> 00:04:02.842
I told the president

00:04:02.842 --> 00:04:03.743
that directly

00:04:03.743 --> 00:04:06.512
when he called me last week on Wednesday

00:04:07.180 --> 00:04:09.682
that this is saying to poor people

00:04:09.682 --> 00:04:11.417
and people who are in need,

00:04:11.417 --> 00:04:12.952
that we don't trust them.

00:04:12.952 --> 00:04:16.022
And the average amount of assistance

00:04:16.022 --> 00:04:18.591
for SNAP, for example, is $6 a day Jake.

00:04:18.791 --> 00:04:21.794
I mean, we're talking about $6 a day.

00:04:21.961 --> 00:04:24.764
And and I think it is really unfortunate

00:04:24.764 --> 00:04:26.833
that the president open the door to this.

00:04:26.833 --> 00:04:29.335
And while at the end of the day,

00:04:29.802 --> 00:04:31.437
perhaps this will

00:04:31.437 --> 00:04:32.672
because of the exemptions,

00:04:32.672 --> 00:04:34.274
perhaps it will be okay.

00:04:34.274 --> 00:04:35.341
I can't commit to that.

00:04:35.341 --> 00:04:37.176
I really don't know.

00:04:37.176 --> 00:04:38.011
And our caucus

00:04:38.011 --> 00:04:39.512
and it's not just the progressives

00:04:39.512 --> 00:04:41.514
across the ideological spectrum,

00:04:41.514 --> 00:04:43.783
including problem solvers, by the way.

00:04:43.783 --> 00:04:46.419
People feel that this is bad policy.

00:04:46.419 --> 00:04:47.854
So so

00:04:47.854 --> 00:04:49.155
it's very unfortunate

00:04:49.155 --> 00:04:50.123
that it's even made its way

00:04:50.123 --> 00:04:50.923
into the discussion.

00:04:50.923 --> 00:04:52.358
So but what I'm hearing from you

00:04:52.358 --> 00:04:54.894
is that if the exemptions are,

00:04:54.894 --> 00:04:56.029
as I described,

00:04:56.029 --> 00:04:59.132
which for homeless for veterans,

00:04:59.432 --> 00:05:00.600
it's possible

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:01.534
that you're willing

00:05:01.534 --> 00:05:03.269
to hold your nose and vote for it

00:05:03.269 --> 00:05:04.470
to avoid a default.

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:05.705
And again,

00:05:05.705 --> 00:05:06.806
it all depends on what you see

00:05:06.806 --> 00:05:07.840
in the tax day.

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:09.542
Nobody should take anything

00:05:09.542 --> 00:05:12.145
as cement as cement it is, right?

00:05:13.846 --> 00:05:14.280
That's right.

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:14.814
No, I just

00:05:14.814 --> 00:05:15.315
don't know,

00:05:15.315 --> 00:05:17.183
because the numbers of people

00:05:17.183 --> 00:05:18.851
that are affected

00:05:18.851 --> 00:05:21.321
are, you know, is is really what this is.

00:05:21.321 --> 00:05:22.388
We have to look at that.

00:05:22.388 --> 00:05:24.490
And if there is

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:25.091
anyway, I don't

00:05:25.091 --> 00:05:26.793
I don't want to get into suppositions

00:05:26.793 --> 00:05:28.127
because I just need to see the text.

00:05:28.127 --> 00:05:29.562
And that's the other thing.

00:05:29.562 --> 00:05:30.997
How is it possible

00:05:30.997 --> 00:05:32.131
that we are at a place

00:05:32.131 --> 00:05:33.199
where the debt ceiling

00:05:33.199 --> 00:05:34.300
and by the way, Jake,

00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:35.968
two countries in the world

00:05:35.968 --> 00:05:36.736
have a debt ceiling.

00:05:36.736 --> 00:05:37.770
The United States

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:41.040
and I think it's Denmark and they do not

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:43.943
they tie it to a percentage of GDP debt,

00:05:43.943 --> 00:05:45.378
to a percentage of GDP.

00:05:45.378 --> 00:05:47.280
And our net interest payments

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:49.115
have actually are below

00:05:49.115 --> 00:05:51.517
what's called 50 years average.

00:05:51.551 --> 00:05:52.752
Why didn't Democrats

00:05:52.752 --> 00:05:53.486
don't tell us why

00:05:53.486 --> 00:05:54.654
we're even in this situation?

00:05:54.654 --> 00:05:54.954
Sure.

00:05:54.954 --> 00:05:55.455
But like,

00:05:55.455 --> 00:05:55.888
you know,

00:05:55.888 --> 00:05:56.589
you guys

00:05:56.589 --> 00:05:57.990
you guys controlled the House, the Senate

00:05:57.990 --> 00:05:59.992
and the White House until until January.

00:05:59.992 --> 00:06:01.361
Why didn't you just take care of

00:06:01.361 --> 00:06:02.695
this in November or December?

00:06:04.497 --> 00:06:05.131
Well, as you

00:06:05.131 --> 00:06:06.799
know, that's what I pushed for.

00:06:06.799 --> 00:06:08.234
That's what the Progressive Caucus put.

00:06:08.234 --> 00:06:09.035
I know. But why the blame?

00:06:09.035 --> 00:06:11.904
But why didn't go that was public?

00:06:11.904 --> 00:06:12.772
Well, I think it's

00:06:12.772 --> 00:06:14.474
because we didn't have 50 votes

00:06:14.474 --> 00:06:15.541
in the Senate.

00:06:15.541 --> 00:06:15.842
You know,

00:06:15.842 --> 00:06:16.642
unfortunately,

00:06:16.642 --> 00:06:18.277
we are also governed

00:06:18.277 --> 00:06:20.880
by a couple of conservative Democrats

00:06:21.414 --> 00:06:22.648
and or independents

00:06:22.648 --> 00:06:23.483
in the Senate

00:06:23.483 --> 00:06:25.585
who refuse to take the actions

00:06:25.585 --> 00:06:27.053
that we need to take.

00:06:27.053 --> 00:06:27.754
And that's

00:06:27.754 --> 00:06:28.388
I think that's

00:06:28.388 --> 00:06:29.822
why we ended up in the situation

00:06:29.822 --> 00:06:30.890
we're in.

00:06:31.023 --> 00:06:32.658
That's why we need a bigger majority

00:06:32.658 --> 00:06:34.160
in the Senate of people

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:35.194
who are actually going

00:06:35.194 --> 00:06:36.062
to continue

00:06:36.062 --> 00:06:37.530
to do what the country needs

00:06:37.530 --> 00:06:38.431
and what our constitutional

00:06:38.431 --> 00:06:39.432
obligation is.

00:06:39.432 --> 00:06:40.733
Because, listen,

00:06:40.733 --> 00:06:42.201
that the debt ceiling

00:06:42.201 --> 00:06:43.669
and we've explained this ad nauseum,

00:06:43.669 --> 00:06:44.670
but I just think it's important

00:06:44.670 --> 00:06:45.905
to say it again,

00:06:45.905 --> 00:06:47.840
we go through an appropriations process.

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:48.941
You know this very well, Jake.

00:06:48.941 --> 00:06:50.476
We go through an appropriations process.

00:06:50.476 --> 00:06:51.411
We argue

00:06:51.411 --> 00:06:53.946
that is where the majority in the House

00:06:53.946 --> 00:06:54.480
and the Senate

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:55.548
get to

00:06:55.848 --> 00:06:57.617
figure out, legislate and negotiate

00:06:57.617 --> 00:07:00.753
what is right and legislate exactly.

00:07:00.753 --> 00:07:02.522
And at the end of that process,

00:07:02.522 --> 00:07:03.623
we come up with something.

00:07:03.623 --> 00:07:05.758
We pass it through a budget

00:07:05.792 --> 00:07:07.794
we pass appropriations bills.

00:07:07.794 --> 00:07:08.928
And the debt

00:07:08.928 --> 00:07:11.798
ceiling is essentially about implementing

00:07:11.964 --> 00:07:13.900
what Congress has already.

00:07:13.900 --> 00:07:15.535
Yeah, no, it's paying for bit

00:07:15.535 --> 00:07:17.103
of money already spent that.

00:07:17.103 --> 00:07:18.337
Yeah. 100%.

00:07:18.337 --> 00:07:18.971
That's right.

00:07:18.971 --> 00:07:21.107
And and I think at the end of the day,

00:07:21.107 --> 00:07:21.941
this is going to make it

00:07:21.941 --> 00:07:23.242
easier, hopefully,

00:07:23.242 --> 00:07:24.210
to take the debt

00:07:24.210 --> 00:07:26.412
ceiling off the table permanently.

00:07:26.446 --> 00:07:27.513
You know, Brendan Boyle,

00:07:27.513 --> 00:07:28.881
our ranking member on budget,

00:07:28.881 --> 00:07:29.749
has a great bill.

00:07:29.749 --> 00:07:31.284
I'm a co-sponsor of that bill

00:07:31.284 --> 00:07:32.351
to give the authority

00:07:32.351 --> 00:07:33.553
to the Treasury secretary

00:07:33.553 --> 00:07:33.953
to be able

00:07:33.953 --> 00:07:34.921
to just raise

00:07:34.921 --> 00:07:35.288
the debt

00:07:35.288 --> 00:07:36.656
ceiling in accordance

00:07:36.656 --> 00:07:39.158
with whatever Congress has passed.

00:07:39.158 --> 00:07:39.692
So clearly,

00:07:39.692 --> 00:07:41.060
all we're saying is Congress

00:07:41.060 --> 00:07:42.028
passes this stuff.

00:07:42.028 --> 00:07:44.197
It should just be raised automatically

00:07:44.464 --> 00:07:45.364
should not allow people

00:07:45.364 --> 00:07:46.799
to continue to take us hostage.

00:07:46.799 --> 00:07:47.333
Quick question

00:07:47.333 --> 00:07:49.135
before you go, Congressman.

00:07:49.135 --> 00:07:49.802
Speaker McCarthy,

00:07:49.802 --> 00:07:51.571
he briefed House Republicans last night.

00:07:51.571 --> 00:07:52.472
House Democrats are

00:07:52.472 --> 00:07:54.474
scheduled to be briefed until five p.

00:07:54.474 --> 00:07:55.775
M later today.

00:07:55.775 --> 00:07:57.376
Have you spoken with Democratic leader

00:07:57.376 --> 00:07:58.978
Jeffries or President Biden

00:07:58.978 --> 00:08:00.446
since the deal was announced last night?

00:08:02.348 --> 00:08:03.483
I have not spoken with

00:08:03.483 --> 00:08:04.484
either of them, though.

00:08:04.484 --> 00:08:06.752
I have been texting with Leader Jeffries.

00:08:07.320 --> 00:08:10.189
However, I did get a lengthy

00:08:10.189 --> 00:08:13.926
briefing from a top White House official.

00:08:13.926 --> 00:08:14.961
Lael Brainard

00:08:14.961 --> 00:08:16.395
called me immediately

00:08:16.395 --> 00:08:16.996
when the

00:08:16.996 --> 00:08:19.665
when the deal was released last night

00:08:19.665 --> 00:08:21.534
to tell me it sounds like

00:08:21.534 --> 00:08:22.201
perhaps not

00:08:22.201 --> 00:08:23.469
everybody was on the same page

00:08:23.469 --> 00:08:24.136
in terms of

00:08:24.136 --> 00:08:27.139
when the deal was going to be announced.

00:08:27.139 --> 00:08:28.241
And, you know,

00:08:28.241 --> 00:08:29.242
I think that

00:08:29.242 --> 00:08:31.143
there was supposed to be a final review

00:08:31.143 --> 00:08:32.545
so I think it got announced

00:08:32.545 --> 00:08:35.448
to Republicans quicker than was expected.

00:08:35.448 --> 00:08:37.450
But I did get a briefing.

00:08:37.450 --> 00:08:39.051
I still have questions, though.

00:08:39.051 --> 00:08:40.286
And at the end of the day,

00:08:40.286 --> 00:08:41.721
I don't like frameworks.

00:08:41.721 --> 00:08:42.688
I think they are

00:08:42.688 --> 00:08:44.457
really problematic in terms

00:08:44.457 --> 00:08:46.359
of being able to make a decision.

00:08:46.359 --> 00:08:47.326
It's fine to say

00:08:47.326 --> 00:08:49.095
we've reached an agreement in principle,

00:08:49.095 --> 00:08:51.264
but all of the text matters

00:08:51.264 --> 00:08:53.199
and there are so many pieces of this

00:08:53.199 --> 00:08:55.001
that we need to look at in terms

00:08:55.001 --> 00:08:57.937
of what the spending is exactly like,

00:08:57.937 --> 00:08:59.639
because my understanding is

00:08:59.639 --> 00:09:01.340
we're essentially held harmless

00:09:01.340 --> 00:09:03.476
at 23 levels.

00:09:03.476 --> 00:09:04.644
It will,

00:09:04.644 --> 00:09:04.944
you know,

00:09:04.944 --> 00:09:06.178
it is still not ideal,

00:09:06.178 --> 00:09:07.547
but not that different

00:09:07.547 --> 00:09:08.948
than what it would be.

00:09:08.948 --> 00:09:09.749
I think for me

00:09:09.749 --> 00:09:11.350
the big questions are around

00:09:11.350 --> 00:09:13.653
what is the what are the changes to NEPA?

00:09:13.653 --> 00:09:15.054
I understand they're pretty minor,

00:09:15.054 --> 00:09:16.422
but I'd like to see those. Yes.

00:09:16.422 --> 00:09:17.723
Then what does this work

00:09:17.723 --> 00:09:18.858
requirements piece look like?

00:09:18.858 --> 00:09:20.126
So I know you hate it when I do this,

00:09:20.126 --> 00:09:20.793
but I'm going to give you a

00:09:20.793 --> 00:09:21.961
yes or no question right now.

00:09:21.961 --> 00:09:22.428
And the way

00:09:22.428 --> 00:09:23.162
yes or no question

00:09:23.162 --> 00:09:24.163
it is all the Democrats.

00:09:24.163 --> 00:09:25.231
I may or may not answer.

00:09:25.231 --> 00:09:25.965
Well, that's fair.

00:09:25.965 --> 00:09:28.100
But Democrats watching right now

00:09:28.234 --> 00:09:29.235
at the White House,

00:09:29.235 --> 00:09:31.404
your friend Hakeem Jeffries, others,

00:09:31.404 --> 00:09:32.939
do they still have to worry

00:09:32.939 --> 00:09:34.840
about the Progressive Caucus

00:09:34.840 --> 00:09:35.741
and whether or not

00:09:35.741 --> 00:09:36.976
your caucus will support.

00:09:36.976 --> 00:09:37.610
Yes, they do.

00:09:37.610 --> 00:09:37.910
Okay.

00:09:37.910 --> 00:09:39.879
Congresswoman Jayapal, thank you so much.

00:09:39.879 --> 00:09:41.614
Have to worry yes, they have to worry.

00:09:41.614 --> 00:09:44.317
Okay. Well, you heard you heard the lady.

00:09:44.550 --> 00:09:45.885
Thank you so much, Congresswoman.

00:09:45.885 --> 00:09:46.953
Good to see you as always.

00:09:48.220 --> 00:09:48.955
Thank you, Jake.

